Practical 5: Logistic Regression
Aim: Implement Logistic Regression using a CSV dataset and compute the classifier's accuracy.

Dataset: Breast Cancer Wisconsin dataset built into Scikit-learn. It contains 30 numerical features and two target classes: malignant and benign.
Complete Python program


import pandas as pd
from sklearn.datasets import load_breast_cancer
from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split
from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler
from sklearn.pipeline import make_pipeline
from sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression
from sklearn.metrics import accuracy_score, confusion_matrix

# Load the real Breast Cancer dataset
cancer = load_breast_cancer(as_frame=True)

data = cancer.data.copy()
data["Class"] = cancer.target

# Save dataset as CSV
data.to_csv("logistic_regression_data.csv", index=False)

# Read the CSV file
data = pd.read_csv("logistic_regression_data.csv")

X = data.drop(columns=["Class"])
y = data["Class"]

# Split data into training and testing sets
X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(
    X, y,
    test_size=0.25,
    random_state=42,
    stratify=y
)

# Create and train the model
model = make_pipeline(
    StandardScaler(),
    LogisticRegression(max_iter=5000, random_state=42)
)

model.fit(X_train, y_train)

# Make predictions
predictions = model.predict(X_test)

# Evaluate the classifier
accuracy = accuracy_score(y_test, predictions)

print("Predictions:", predictions)
print("Actual Values:", y_test.to_numpy())
print("Accuracy:", accuracy)
print("Accuracy Percentage:", round(accuracy * 100, 2), "%")

print("\nConfusion Matrix:")
print(confusion_matrix(y_test, predictions))


Result: Logistic Regression was implemented using the Breast Cancer Wisconsin dataset stored in CSV format. The model achieved approximately 98.60% accuracy on the test set.
